macOS x86_64: swap GSBASE between the TEB and the host thread block at every PE crossing.

Port of upstream Wine 3a16aabbf5 (Brendan Shanks, "ntdll: On macOS x86_64, swap GSBASE between the
TEB and macOS TSD when entering/leaving PE code", in Wine 10.5) with its follow-ups folded in (no swap
in user_mode_callback_return, 3aa28e45201), written against the CrossOver 26.3 tree (Wine 11.0), which
carries the older design instead: %gs stays the host pthread block for PE code too and three TEB fields
(Tib.Self, ThreadLocalStoragePointer, Peb) are copied into it, plus the Instrumentation[0] hack that
keeps the copy of the TLS pointer in step.

With this patch PE code runs with %gs = the real TEB and Unix code with %gs = the host block, switched
with the private _thread_set_tsd_base trap (0x3000003, works under Rosetta, which has no FSGSBASE) in
the syscall dispatcher, the unix-call dispatcher, call_user_mode_callback and the signal handlers
(init_handler switches to the host block before any pthread call, leave_handler switches back when
returning to PE code). Unix C code never needed %gs:0x30: NtCurrentTeb() in the Unix library is
pthread_getspecific(teb_key). So every %gs offset a Windows program uses is the TEB's: the fiber
pointer at 0x20, the last error at 0x68, the stack bounds at 0x08/0x10, TLS at 0x58.

Why: Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 runs its own fiber system and keeps its state through %gs. On
the mirrored design the stack bounds it sets never reach Wine's TEB, so the exception dispatcher refuses
every frame on a fiber stack ("invalid frame ... unable to dispatch exception" at BOOT_INIT), and a
fiber pointer kept in %gs:0x20 is overwritten at every return to PE code by the engine's own slot
swapping (patch 0009), which ends in the sim's "FiberError" assertion. Measured on an M4, macOS 27.0,
engine r18 with D3DMetal 4.0b2, 2026-10-08 (gin/private/notes/msfs-2024-2026-10-08.md).

Retires patches 0008 (mirror Tib.FiberData), 0009 (swap the fiber pointer and the QoS slot), 0011 and
0016 (last error in %gs:0x68): with %gs = TEB in PE code those slots are the TEB's own fields and the
host QoS word is untouched, since host code only runs with %gs = the host block.

Known limit, same as upstream before 0f82d28780d: a signal taken on a thread without a TEB (a host
library's own thread) computes its TEB from the stack and must not happen; the old design crashed there
as well (NULL thread data).

Trial build for the MSFS 2024 investigation, branch msfs-gsbase. Not verified yet when written.
